These are common phenomena arising out of spinlocks (by spinlock I mean a pure and dirty unfair CAS loop)

Q: Why would I have such a lock around my code?
A: Read "spinlocks" for details but the short answer is because it is very tempting.

Q: Huh?
A: Consider I have a readFloat / multiplyBy 4.344123f / writeFloat. Is this small enough to be surrounded by a
   spinlock? (I m hoping nobody is dumb enough to surround calls that can visibly block with spinlocsk btw)

Q: Yea sure.
A: Trouble is as explained in the chapter "spinlocks" critical sections cascade so the effective critical section
   is 30x for a highly contended spinlock.


With the above background in mind there are two problems that a simple dumb unfair spinlock can cause no matter
how small the critical section is. 

The first one has to do with REDUCTION OF BANDWIDTH (aka `n' threads take way toooo long to complete)

The second one has to do with THREAD STARVATION.

===========================================================================================================================
<A> THE UNDERGROUND EXPLOSION PROBLEM: (somewhat related to the THUNDERING HERD PROBLEM)

1250 threads suddenly go for your lock almost at the same time (gets worse with multicores). Its a jungle out there now,
especially if the thread that is in the critical section gets preempted. 

Now there are 1249 idiots asking for the lock and only 1 thread that can do something about it.

But since these are not ordered the worst case could be where the smart thread gets
scheduled behind 1249 threads each taking a full quantum asking the same stupid question over and over again viz.,
"Can I have the lock please?". 
"Heck NO you cant have the lock because I havent released it yet and that has to do with the fact that you re taking
 my seat on the core"

Ok so this thread releases the lock but everytime a thread holding its lock gets preempted (or
faults or whatever ...) you could theoretically argue it could get scheduled again behind the remaining spinning threads. 
Alrite your shot at timeslice gets better as you add more cores but you get the idea.


<B> THE AVALANCHE PROBLEM:

Remember that day when you woke up from the wrong side of the bed, used the wrong shaving cream and drove to work using a
detour and bad things happened? Yes thats the day we re talking of here. 

It all starts slowly 3 threads trying to grab the lock
one wins, completes, other one gets it too but suddently 2 more threads come along. Mr.3 (lets call him that henceforth) never
really makes it all the way. Why? Because this is an open system (meaning the #contenders never becomes zero but its not an 
explosion like <A>) either.

So the net effect is that after 30 mins Mr.3 is still waiting to do a multiple of float by 3 err..sorry by 4.344123f!! Ok
is it really so bad wait another 30mins and he ll get his chance.

Perhaps but thats not the point, Mr.3 as it turns out is a really important thread the result of his multiplication is holding
up 200 other threads which are now cascading and grinding certain subsystems to a halt.

Heres a live example, my fair sleep lock internally used to use a simple CAS instruction for the turnstile. So it means
sleepLock and sleepUnlock both use the same CAS to add and remove a thread from the fifo queue. There was also a getNumContenders
that used the same lock but thats easy to fix with volatile you say, fair enough.

Now as it turns out the sleepUnlock was losing it out to the slow but steady incoming threads trying to sleepLock. As a result
the sleep lock was stuck the queue was building up. Now this is a matter of chance right?
