CONCLUSION:
The moral of the story is:
<a> unfair spinlock around a baby piece of code is a myth, critical sections can never be small enough as it turns out.

<b> The smallest granularity of locking you use in your concurrent library cannot be an unfair spinlock, any fair lock(s)
    you build out of it will face the above two problems.

<c> My solution to the above problem is to use spin + expanding_backoff where you spin a lil sleep a lil next time spin 
    again but sleep a lil more (bounded at the top). You could make this conditional based on the #contenders for the lock
    so the simple case doesnt suffer.

<d> This solves <A> you say not <B>. Correct you also need priority levels where each time a lock spins trying to get the
    lock it checks if its gotten too old to be spinning with the crowd. If yes it raises the locks priority level and 
    next time somebody unlocks he doesnt unlock he unlocks the next level so all old folks can go and get the lock.

<e> A really cool solution would be to instrument the kernel to let a thread tell the OS I m spinning on this resource.
    If you were to ever preempt me or switch me out for whatever reasons, add me to xyz waitqueue before you schedule me again.
    There you go, you have order so no starvation and you ve used your timeslice well but no more. Though even if 1249 threads
    took one quantum and no more it could still give that 1 lock holding thread a tough time but its a one time thing so
    big deal!
